WEBVTT

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.690
How many bids did you open this week,
just to find out they weren't a match?

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:10.270
Government opportunities are scattered
across more than 18 platforms.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.390
Federal, provincial, municipal,
agencies, health and education.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:16.390
And the alerts never stop.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.290
Every one of them looks
like it could be the one.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.210
So you open it.

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:24.890
You log in. You download the documents.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:31.330
You read page after page, until page 47
tells you the site visit was last week.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:32.750
Not a match.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:34.050
Next one.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.030
The bond is more than you can carry.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.410
Not a match.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:39.710
Next one.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.990
It's outside your region.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.650
Not a match.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.510
By Friday afternoon, the whole week is gone.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:48.570
Open. Download.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:49.870
Read.

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:51.170
Not a match.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:53.750
A full week of searching.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.990
Not one bid worth writing.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:00.930
Every hour on the wrong bid is an
hour taken from one you could win.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:06.810
And 78% of construction firms say
estimating roles are hard to fill.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.600
You focus on winning contracts.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.680
I focus on finding them.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.710
I monitor the platforms for you.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.130
I read the documents.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:24.590
And I qualify every opportunity against
your trades, your capacity, your coverage

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:28.250
area, the deadlines, and
the mandatory requirements.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.090
I track every amendment.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.050
You only see what fits.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.220
Each one reaches you already read.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.300
A plain language summary, a link to the
source, and what you need to make the call.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.020
Bid or pass.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:49.980
I spent three years inside the eprocurement
industry, handling more than 17,500

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.540
contractor accounts across
Canada and the US.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.560
I saw where contractors lose bids,
not at the proposal, at the search.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.360
Stop searching.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.700
Start bidding.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.480
Book a discovery call at phildave.com.
